  "query_preview": "..\n\nOngoing convection across northern IL/IN is tied to the arrival of a \ncentral Plains shortwave that will slide along MI's southern border \nover the rest of the morning/early afternoon. While the majority of \nconvection is again expected south of the state where the stalled \nfrontal boundary resides, reinforcing moisture and modest mid-level \nascent supports scattered AM shower chances over the southern half \nof SE MI. Thunder chances during the morning activity will be quite \nlimited due to the lack of available instability (MUCAPE <500J/kg). \nThis gradually changes by afternoon however as ongoing moisture \nadvection pushes surface dewpoints into the the lower 70s allowing \ndiurnal heating to generate MLCAPE between 1000-1500J/kg. Increasing \ninstability combined with the modest synoptic support from the \npassing shortwave supports scattered convective showers and \nthunderstorms through the afternoon, mainly along/south of I-69 \ngiven the location of the wave. Severe storms are unlikely as weak \ncolumn winds keep shear sub-30kts in addition to abysmal mid-level \nlapse aob 6 C/km. PWATs in excess of 2\" and warm cloud layers near \n15kft however do offer the threat of torrential rainfall. That said, \nthe progressive nature of any convection plus the dry summer thus \nfar should keep flooding more localized to the typical floodprone \nurban/low lying areas. Storm chances wane by early evening in the \nsouth as the shortwave departs east; though the arrival of a cold \nfront dropping out of northern MI shifts scattered shower chances \ntowards the northern half of SE MI, particularly the Thumb, this \nevening-early tonight. Late arrival in the diurnal window likely \nkeeps thunder more isolated. \n\nPassage of the aforementioned tonight ushers in a more seasonably \naverage airmass as dewpoints and 850mb temps return to around 60F \nand lower teens C, respectively. As such, pleasant early week in \nstore with highs in the lower to mid 80s Monday-Tuesday and sunnier \nskies.\n\nBroad Hudson Bay upper trough sends another cold front into the \nGreat Lakes midweek. Currently, frontal timing and associated \npreceding showers favored to work across SE MI late Tuesday night-\nWednesday. Cooler air follows to close out the work week with 850mb \ntemps hovering around 10C keeping highs slightly below normal by the \nlatter part of August standards.",
